1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,512
The Search for Exoplanets.

2
00:00:02,462 --> 00:00:07,600
Before 1992, no planets were confirmed beyond the Solar System.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:20,012
That year, astronomers Alexander Wolszczan and Dale Frail announced the discovery of two planets orbiting the millisecond pulsar PSR B1257+12, using pulsar timing variations.

4
00:00:20,012 --> 00:00:29,400
As of July 2026, over 5,800 exoplanets have been confirmed, with more than 10,000 additional candidates awaiting confirmation.

5
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:32,650
Two indirect detection methods dominate.

6
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:45,762
The transit method monitors stellar luminosity for periodic dips; when a planet passes across its host star's face, it blocks 0.01-1% of the star's light, depending on the planet's size relative to the star.

7
00:00:45,762 --> 00:00:59,775
The NASA Kepler Space Telescope, launched in 2009, used this technique across a 115-square-degree field in Cygnus, discovering over 2,600 confirmed exoplanets before its retirement in 2018.

8
00:00:59,775 --> 00:01:17,012
The radial velocity (Doppler wobble) method detects the star's periodic motion around the system's barycenter via spectral line shifts; the first exoplanet around a Sun-like star, 51 Pegasi b, was discovered this way in 1995 by Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz.

9
00:01:17,012 --> 00:01:26,300
The Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite (TESS), launched in 2018, surveys nearly the entire sky for transits around bright stars.

10
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:35,825
Exoplanets range from sub-Earth-sized rocky bodies (Kepler-37b, smaller than Mercury) to super-Jupiters with orbital periods of days.

11
00:01:35,825 --> 00:01:43,475
Approximately 1 in 5 Sun-like stars hosts an Earth-sized planet in the habitable zone, where temperatures permit liquid water.

12
00:01:43,475 --> 00:02:00,112
The James Webb Space Telescope, launched in 2021, performs transmission spectroscopy of exoplanet atmospheres—measuring the absorption spectrum of starlight filtered through planetary air—searching for biosignature gases such as molecular oxygen, methane, and water vapor.

13
00:02:00,112 --> 00:02:08,762
The TRAPPIST-1 system, seven Earth-sized planets three of which orbit in the habitable zone, is a prime target for atmospheric characterization.

